WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.675
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.675 --> 00:00:04.800
SOROUSH RADPOUR: Thank you.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:05.976
Hi, everyone.

00:00:05.976 --> 00:00:09.540
I'm Soroush and
let's get it started.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:14.110
I'm going to focus on
Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:00:14.110 --> 00:00:18.990
It is our product for helping
developers go from prototyping

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:24.420
to a real production system.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:27.090
So the agenda for
today is I'm going

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:30.900
to give you an overview
of our motivation

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.905
and our approach for how we
build Kubeflow Pipelines,

00:00:35.905 --> 00:00:38.460
and then I'm going
to give you some

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.760
of the examples of the
problems that inspired Kubeflow

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.420
Pipelines and what we heard from
the customers and their needs,

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:49.500
and last, I will show
you some of the features

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.540
that Kubeflow
Pipelines has currently

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:55.930
and how you can use it.

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:59.680
So let's go through
the motivation first.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:05.280
So if you have worked on machine
learning deployments before,

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.580
you know that building
and deploying ML models

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:09.495
is hard and costly.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:19.020
In order to go end-to-end from
grabbing the data to having

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:22.608
the model serving and so on,
there is much more than just

00:01:22.608 --> 00:01:23.400
building the model.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:31.020
So the problems that we
see today in AI are--

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:34.440
at least three of them that
we are trying to tackle

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:38.640
is deployment, where there
are different solutions

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.830
for different frameworks.

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:47.520
And each cloud
provider might want

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:53.580
you to use their solution
for a specific AI problem,

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.920
and then when you
want to go to on-prem

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.440
or switch between clouds,
they usually break

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:02.010
or they're not even supported.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.740
So the next one
is finding talent

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:09.690
and really using the
talent's time effectively.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:13.050
ML expertise is
not easy to come by

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:19.050
and you want to really
use their ML expert time

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:22.240
as efficiently as possible.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:26.075
And lastly, the ecosystem is--

00:02:26.075 --> 00:02:27.450
the problem with
the ecosystem is

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:32.070
that it is difficult to find
and reuse what is out there.

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:35.520
If you know that a new model
came out for image processing,

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.530
for example, and you
want to grab that model

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:43.530
and use it in your
production system,

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:46.410
finding it and using it
in your production system

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.700
takes a lot of effort.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:51.620
So our solution
to these problems,

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:55.200
it is Kubeflow, reusable
pipelines, and AI Hub.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.260
So I'm going to go
through these one by one.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.170
So what is Kubeflow?

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:08.550
It is built on top of
Kubernetes to let you train

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:10.620
your machine learning models.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:16.110
It has support for PyTorch,
TensorFlow, XGBoost,

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:19.860
and a lot of other
popular frameworks.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:23.560
It also takes care
of serving and so on,

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:26.200
so it is more end-to-end.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:31.110
It has support for GPU
and multi-node training

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:35.130
so it can scale up or down
depending on your problem.

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:39.500
And you can run it on any
Kubernetes deployment,

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:43.480
so if you have your own
cluster at your company,

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:46.260
you don't want to
use GCP, great--

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:48.010
if you have a
Kubernetes deployment,

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:52.300
you can install
Kubeflow on top of it--

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:54.390
but there is also GCP support.

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:00.900
Next is reusable pipelines.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:06.990
The goal is basically enabling
an engineer or a machine

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:09.780
learning expert
to not waste time

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:12.460
on rebuilding and reinventing
everything from scratch

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:16.079
but picking the pieces
that were built before

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:20.519
and really just reusing and
building what they care about.

00:04:24.790 --> 00:04:27.570
So discoverability of,
like, are reusable pipelines

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:32.780
is the problem, and AI
Hub tries to tackle that.

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:38.100
AI Hub is a product
that Google has

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:41.700
built that you can search
for the type of problem

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:47.040
you're trying to solve and
find solutions that are already

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.350
built for that type of problem.

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:53.670
And very easily build your
pipeline around those.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:57.800
So you can stitch them together
and build your pipeline there.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:00.820
It is really fast
and easy to use.

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:03.290
There is a one-click
installation

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:04.640
for Kubeflow on GCP.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:07.430
So if you found
something that you

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:12.290
want to use from the AI Hub,
you can easily deploy it

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:18.500
on your GCP account, basically.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:20.940
There is also
enterprise-level sharing.

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:25.010
So what I mean is that,
within your company,

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:27.110
if you have built
something that is

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:31.800
interesting to other teams,
other employees of the company,

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:36.300
you can share that solution
with the rest of the company.

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:40.100
And also if you want
to share it publicly,

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.830
you can also say I want to share
this publicly so everyone can

00:05:42.830 --> 00:05:43.330
use it.

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:52.260
So next I'm going to talk
about the type of problems that

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:54.404
inspired Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:03.780
Let's say you have your problem
that you are trying to solve.

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:07.570
You figure out where
you can gather the data

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:11.690
types that you care about
for this type of problem.

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:15.190
You create your CSV file,
you do your pre-processing

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:20.470
of that file and have your
Python for pre-processing,

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:22.720
and then you have
another Python file

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:27.440
for like training the model and
it will produce a model file.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:29.200
And you are trying
to figure out,

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:32.230
how do you go from
that model file

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:35.710
to something you can
have in the production?

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:38.830
And soon you will
see there is more

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:43.780
to that, like once I
go beyond the ML model,

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:47.620
you might want to look at
how the model quality changes

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:48.210
over time.

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:52.840
If you just put the model
out, is the model still

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:57.432
as performant as it was
when you first deploy it?

00:06:57.432 --> 00:06:58.390
So there is monitoring.

00:06:58.390 --> 00:07:02.590
And then you think about,
how can I continuously deploy

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:03.100
this model?

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:05.690
So with the first year
that it is coming in,

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:08.830
I want to create a new
model, push that new model

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:10.430
to the production.

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:13.970
How do I monitor new
versions of the model?

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:17.740
How do I scale the
serving of the model

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:20.860
so that, like, if there
is new traffic coming in,

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:22.450
I can easily respond to that.

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:26.920
When the new data
is coming in, you

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:28.888
want to verify the
new data so that like,

00:07:28.888 --> 00:07:31.180
if there is a regression
somewhere in the pipeline that

00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:35.690
produces that input data, you
want to be able to catch that.

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:38.200
So as you were
looking through it,

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:42.500
it can grow to really
a hard problem.

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:46.420
So this is a tweet
that talks about

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:48.790
like, she developed
the model in three

00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:51.890
weeks but going to a production
system took almost a year.

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:59.560
So what we hear from customers
from different companies

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:04.990
is that using these tools
for workflow management

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:08.780
for pipeline management that are
not built for machine learning,

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:13.713
they have trouble
applying it to machine

00:08:13.713 --> 00:08:15.130
learning because
there are machine

00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:16.330
learning specific things.

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:19.600
For example, you care about
metrics in machine learning.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:21.280
For each model, that
can be different.

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:25.450
You care about experimenting
with different hyperparameters

00:08:25.450 --> 00:08:28.670
and different model
versions are different

00:08:28.670 --> 00:08:34.210
and there are things that
are very specific to machine

00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:38.330
learning that you can't just use
any pipeline management system.

00:08:40.701 --> 00:08:42.909
And if you don't have any
pipeline management system,

00:08:42.909 --> 00:08:46.640
then your models might
be working today,

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:50.620
but as the data changes and
the user behavior changes,

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:53.680
the models become stale
and updating the model

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:55.600
might take many, many steps.

00:08:55.600 --> 00:09:02.490
You might have to run few
jobs manually to grab the data

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:06.610
and train the model and test
to make sure it is actually

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:10.310
performing as you expect and
then push it to production.

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:17.020
And if you want to do it every
week, it is a lot of overhead.

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:23.170
And lastly, experimentation
of different hyperparameters

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:29.290
is difficult. There is
no good way of tracking

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:32.990
what was the hyperparameter
that was used for this model.

00:09:32.990 --> 00:09:36.130
So if in the future, like
a month from the time

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:37.900
that the model was
trained, you want

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:42.370
to look back and see how was
this model trained, tracking

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:45.850
that and being able to
reproduce the same model

00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:49.510
or change your
model is difficult.

00:09:49.510 --> 00:09:54.340
And these are the
kind of problems

00:09:54.340 --> 00:09:56.590
that the customers had.

00:09:56.590 --> 00:10:00.460
So that is why we built
Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:10:00.460 --> 00:10:03.520
So next, I'll give
you an overview

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:08.760
of the Kubeflow Pipelines.

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:11.980
The features it has.

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:16.520
The first piece of it is
orchestrating your system.

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:28.590
Basically, in your pipeline, you
will have different components

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:34.500
that are responsible
for peer processing

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:38.310
here or transforming,
training, and so on.

00:10:38.310 --> 00:10:43.860
And Kubeflow Pipeline is
responsible for making sure

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:47.310
that the dependencies
are met and it

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:51.750
will execute the containers
in the right order

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:54.240
and takes care of
distribution of them.

00:10:57.160 --> 00:11:01.890
You can also take a
look at the trainer job

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:05.050
and look at the metrics
that it provides.

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:07.890
So if you click on
the trainer, you

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:10.230
can see the ROC
curve in this case

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:13.110
that the trainer was providing.

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:15.495
If it was a TensorFlow job,
it will have a TensorBoard

00:11:15.495 --> 00:11:18.900
if you have used
TensorFlow before.

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:21.840
But if it is like
XGBoost model, it

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:26.580
will have its own
visualizations of its metrics.

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:29.130
You can also look at the
logs during training.

00:11:29.130 --> 00:11:31.170
If there was any
errors or something,

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:32.950
you can take a look here.

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:37.810
And the configuration that
was used for the trainer.

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:41.565
So as I said, the Pipeline
is basically a set of tasks--

00:11:41.565 --> 00:11:47.190
ML tasks-- and we use
containers to encapsulate

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:49.410
all it needs to run.

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:53.920
Make the code portable
and repeatable.

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:58.650
A task can be run on a single
machine or a distributed

00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:01.410
fashion, and Kubeflow
will take care of that.

00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:05.610
And there is a
Python SDK that lets

00:12:05.610 --> 00:12:10.230
you specify the steps that
your pipeline needs to run

00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:13.380
and the dependencies
between the different steps.

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:18.060
And it provides a
way for you to say

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:21.300
what are the parameters
to these pipelines.

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:23.160
An example of a
parameter to a pipeline

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:29.820
can be like hyperparameters
for learning rates

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:34.710
or for the optimizer to use
or it can be the input files

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:39.240
that you want to process or what
is the column in your CSV file

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:40.970
that has the labeling.

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:46.410
So it will be easier to
reuse if you parameterize it.

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:51.180
Here is an example
of a Jupyter notebook

00:12:51.180 --> 00:12:58.170
that you have input
your pipeline here.

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:02.910
There is peer processing,
training and analysis,

00:13:02.910 --> 00:13:05.580
and prediction and deploy steps.

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:12.390
And it is just as easy as
a few lines of Python code

00:13:12.390 --> 00:13:16.390
to build a pipeline and run
it on the Kubeflow cluster.

00:13:21.470 --> 00:13:25.490
Next is how you
reliably experiment

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:31.430
and you can rapidly change
and develop your model.

00:13:31.430 --> 00:13:35.450
So the cycle for
experimentation is basically

00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:40.070
that you pick some
hyperparameters

00:13:40.070 --> 00:13:41.750
that you want to try.

00:13:41.750 --> 00:13:45.870
Let's say I want to try 100
trees, 200 trees, and so on.

00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:50.540
And you submit a bunch of runs.

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:51.920
You wait for the results.

00:13:51.920 --> 00:13:55.850
You get some outputs
out of those results

00:13:55.850 --> 00:14:02.390
and now you want to look
at which one of these runs

00:14:02.390 --> 00:14:06.200
had the best results and
maybe clone the best one,

00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:09.710
modify it, and run it again
with different configuration,

00:14:09.710 --> 00:14:13.340
maybe slightly
differently, and experiment

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:17.310
and improve your model.

00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:20.710
So this is the UI for
tracking your models.

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:26.300
You can see that
all the runs are

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:28.280
grouped by their experiments.

00:14:28.280 --> 00:14:31.610
So if you're experimenting
with like an image model,

00:14:31.610 --> 00:14:34.280
and like here, there's
a TFMA experiment,

00:14:34.280 --> 00:14:35.930
XGBoost train experiment.

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:39.290
And they can have
multiple runs, and these

00:14:39.290 --> 00:14:42.050
are previous runs that finished
and there is a currently

00:14:42.050 --> 00:14:44.930
running job here.

00:14:44.930 --> 00:14:51.560
And you can easily
clone an existing run.

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:56.100
So you open one of the runs,
you see that it has a good ROC,

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:59.660
you can click on the Clone here
or look at the configuration

00:14:59.660 --> 00:15:03.110
and create a copy of it.

00:15:03.110 --> 00:15:05.010
And when you create
a copy of it,

00:15:05.010 --> 00:15:08.180
it will fill out the
parameters that you provided,

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:11.420
but you can update
the parameters.

00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:16.730
So you will say here, instead
of like number of trees, 200,

00:15:16.730 --> 00:15:18.650
you want 300 trees this time.

00:15:18.650 --> 00:15:22.310
And it is just as easy
to iterate on your model.

00:15:26.580 --> 00:15:30.300
So comparing between
runs is very easy.

00:15:30.300 --> 00:15:31.470
It will give you--

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:37.920
basically for an experiment,
you will see a list of metrics

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:44.250
and you can compare different
runs and how they performed.

00:15:44.250 --> 00:15:47.460
And if you want to dig
deeper into each of these,

00:15:47.460 --> 00:15:50.700
you can select, let's
say, like three of them

00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:54.210
and click on Compare Runs.

00:15:54.210 --> 00:15:57.750
And that way, you'll get
a deep dive on those three

00:15:57.750 --> 00:15:59.670
how they compare
against each other.

00:16:05.080 --> 00:16:05.770
Yeah.

00:16:05.770 --> 00:16:08.020
You get the metrics,
AUC, and so on.

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:15.410
The last piece of the puzzle was
sharing and reusing components.

00:16:18.860 --> 00:16:21.170
So as we said, we
wanted to reuse

00:16:21.170 --> 00:16:26.510
and compose instead of
reimplementing and reinventing

00:16:26.510 --> 00:16:27.411
everything.

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:35.300
So in this example
that we saw previously,

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:39.290
these steps are actually
coming from AI Hub.

00:16:39.290 --> 00:16:43.700
So once you've found
the right component that

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:47.900
does what you care about,
calling it in your pipeline

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:51.200
is as easy as just calling the
function in your Python code.

00:16:51.200 --> 00:16:56.900
And we find the
containers that needs

00:16:56.900 --> 00:17:00.050
to run as part of
this AI Hub component

00:17:00.050 --> 00:17:02.915
and we'll add it to your
pipeline, basically.

00:17:08.859 --> 00:17:11.960
Yeah, that's all there is.

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:13.400
Yeah.

00:17:13.400 --> 00:17:15.806
Any questions now?

00:17:15.806 --> 00:17:18.740
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:21.027 --> 00:17:22.652
AUDIENCE: What is
the backend for this?

00:17:22.652 --> 00:17:24.637
[INAUDIBLE]

00:17:24.637 --> 00:17:26.470
SOROUSH RADPOUR: There
is a combination of--

00:17:26.470 --> 00:17:27.530
AUDIENCE: Can you
